{"candidate_ids":["C005","C001"],"rationale":"Preferred candidate C005 (Ni 1.0, Er 0.4, Zr 0.8 at.%) is recorded on the task’s J-first basis. Exact coarsening evidence B005:C005:coarsening gives J=0.2780451852670357. This is a practical tie with C004 at B004:C004:coarsening J=0.27804518508374554 and with C003 at B002:C003:coarsening J=0.27804518493703073 because all |ΔJ| are far below the 0.0005 rule, while C001 is meaningfully worse at B002:C001:coarsening J=0.3052704942407164, a disadvantage of 0.02722530897368071. Within that tied low-J set, C005 is preferred over C004 and C003 by measured secondary tradeoffs, not by a hidden score: versus C004 on the controlled fixed-Er=0.4, fixed-Zr=0.8 branch, B006:C005:phase_stability and B003:C004:phase_stability show both pass L12_660<=1.05 with C005 L12_660=0.9688025394017767 and C004 L12_660=0.9392845531138507, while L12_250 is essentially unchanged (3.310623672846295 vs 3.3106236725471097). On the same matched branch, B007:C005:solidification versus B007:C004:solidification shows nearly unchanged terminal ternary reservoir (3.140411170021904 vs 3.147752611366376 mol%) but much lower Al3Ni for C005 (1.3943980755583592 vs 3.3268069681600134 mol%) and slightly lower FR_K/HCS (269.6775 vs 273.43437 K; 0.50373060225 vs 0.5107480597230001) with identical CSC=0.18679. Versus C003, B001:C003:phase_stability shows essentially the same L12_250 but much higher competing Al3Zr burden than C005: Al3Zr_250=3.0883286636031673 and Al3Zr_660=4.145856925602474 for C003 versus 1.48828427253575 and 2.5493239758995703 for C005, and a higher Al3Zr upper disappearance boundary 1101.5625 C versus 1035.9375 C. All cited upper boundaries have liquid_at_upper_boundary=true, so they are reported only as liquid-involved upper disappearance behavior, not solid-state solvus values.

Balanced alternative C001 (Ni 1.5, Er 0.4, Zr 0.4 at.%) is justified explicitly as a worse-J tradeoff alloy, not as a coarsening-first contender. B001:C001:phase_stability shows it is feasible with L12_250=3.1990436089735828, L12_660=0.8923485574934417, Al3Zr_250=0, Al3Zr_660=0.9968651939905895, boundary=945.3125000000001 C and liquid_at_upper_boundary=true. B008:C001:solidification shows why it merits shortlist status despite worse J: terminal ternary remains essentially the same as C005 (3.1551824611524517 vs 3.140411170021904 mol%), while terminal Al3Zr is much lower (1.4529790160225757 vs 3.039659280781794 mol%), FR_K is dramatically lower (9.98812 vs 269.6775 K), and HCS is dramatically lower (0.01916220822 vs 0.50373060225), although CSC is slightly worse than C005 (0.19185 vs 0.18679) and terminal Al3Ni is higher (3.3170750587487516 vs 1.3943980755583592). C001 therefore fits only as a balanced alternative emphasizing far better solidification proxies and lower equilibrium/terminal Al3Zr burden at the cost of a meaningful J penalty and some Al3Ni increase. It is not supported for preferred status because its Er/Zr branch lacks the required controlled Ni comparison: only Ni=1.5 has been measured on the Er=0.4, Zr=0.4 branch, so R3 branch-specific Ni support exists for C004/C005 but not for elevating C001 above the coarsening-first branch.

Accordingly, record shortlist structure as: preferred C005 for coarsening-first selection among measured phase-feasible candidates, and balanced alternative C001 for users prioritizing substantially better FR/HCS and lower Al3Zr burden while accepting worse J and higher Al3Ni."}
